  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAnomalously warm temperatures continue to provide well above\nnormal daytime highs for the region through Friday. With strong\nhigh pressure moving overhead into the weekend, daytime highs\nwill tend to be not as warm. Overnight temperatures are \nexpected to be relatively cool, falling off in the evening as \ndaytime heating and mixing ceases. Valley locations continue to\nimpress with their daily temp range, falling into the lower 30s\nto mid 20s by daybreak. \n\nTonight shouldn't be much different than nights previous. There\nwill be periods of cirrus moving overhead, but current satellite shows\nthis as fairly thin to the west. Another late night/early\nmorning fog is expected along the CT River Valley, slowly\ndissipating Friday morning.\n\nClouds become a bigger factor in overnight lows and daytime\nhighs come Sunday as they advance ahead of low pressure moving\nup the East Coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe next measurable rainfall is likely Sunday night through\nMonday as low pressure advances north along the East Coast.\nThere remains variance in the low's location and timing as it\nadvances towards New England/the Gulf of Maine, but all\nsolutions keep rain in the forecast for the forecast area's coast\nand interior.\n\nEnsemble low centers are still splayed wide, with little\nagreement. This is particularly notable in the EPS as the system\npushes north of the Carolinas. Therefore expect more confidence\nin rainfall amounts and timing once the low develops during the\nfirst half of the weekend. Current NBM guidance has increased \nrapidly for totals over the past 24 hours as more members shift \nwest. This would put totals greater than an inch for a sizable \nportion of the eastern forecast area. Most early low centers \nfavor these greater amounts, because the incoming trough is \nfurther west and allows a more westerly track. This is compared\nto an escape into the open Atlantic that is flatter and further\noffshore.\n\nWill continue to monitor the low's forecast track, as an inland\ntrack could open the coast up for LLJ winds and a potential\ngusty wind hazard. At this time however, this is unlikely given\nthe plume of ensemble member tracks into the Gulf of Maine or\nfurther east.\n\nThe active pattern may well continue into mid week towards the\nThanksgiving holiday as long range guidance signals the chance\nfor additional low pressure organizing in the eastern CONUS and\nmoving up the coast. Solutions are even wider with this\nproposition, so confidence is low, but this will overlap with\nthe monthly high astronomical tide cycle. The high tides will\nmake the coast susceptible to coastal flooding if combined with\nswell and surge from a coastal low. Those with interests along\nthe coast should monitor coming forecasts should this threat\nbecome more likely.",
